WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:05.090
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:06.490
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:08.050
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:09.310
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:13.470
And now we're doing the ultimate Office Lovers podcast just for you.

6
00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:17.970
Each week, we will dive deeper into the world of The Office with exclusive interviews,

7
00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:21.170
behind-the-scenes details, and lots of BFF stories.

8
00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:23.190
We're the Office Lady 6.0.

9
00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:28.640
Happy New Year.

10
00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.000
Happy 2026.

11
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:32.000
2026, lady.

12
00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:33.940
We have a New Year's resolution.

13
00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:34.860
We do.

14
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:35.740
Should we share it?

15
00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:41.500
Yes, this is a shared BFF, not only just in our work life, but in our home life,

16
00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:43.000
shared New Year's resolution.

17
00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.580
I've never had a shared New Year's resolution before.

18
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:45.980
Me either.

19
00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:49.160
I highly recommend it because I feel like we're going to hold each other accountable.

20
00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:49.820
I do too.

21
00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:51.040
Okay, say what it is.

22
00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.760
It's called get off the hamster wheel.

23
00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:54.520
That's right.

24
00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:59.200
Because we work from home, and I've talked to a lot of people who manage businesses from home,

25
00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:04.620
you can very easily always be working or always be doing a chore.

26
00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:08.440
Or it's like, oh, I'm just going to throw this laundry in before I get on this Zoom.

27
00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:08.920
Yeah.

28
00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:13.620
Or it's Saturday, so I'm just going to bang out a couple hours of work here in the morning.

29
00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.680
But what happens is, you were saying, Ange, it's like, all of a sudden you wake up and

30
00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:23.040
you're like, I have worked 20 days in a row without a full day off.

31
00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:27.180
Yeah, because you just like, you know, you just scooch little things in every day.

32
00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:32.440
But what you were saying, and I so agree, is that let's say you're like, oh, you know,

33
00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:33.180
it's Saturday morning.

34
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.460
I'm going to do a few hours of work.

35
00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:35.980
But then it stays with you.

36
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:36.600
All day.

37
00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:37.040
All day.

38
00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:39.480
So then you're like, hmm, I wonder if Jenna saw that email.

39
00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:40.240
And I'm like, no, no, no.

40
00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:41.540
Turn that part of my brain off.

41
00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:42.040
Yeah.

42
00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:47.420
So I am committed to you, Jenna, that we are going to take two days off every week.

43
00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:48.160
Yes.

44
00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:49.420
Weekends off.

45
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:50.660
Yes.

46
00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:54.020
We're getting off the hamster wheel for a full decompress.

47
00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.860
And this was inspired by our break, where we took a full week off for our break.

48
00:01:58.960 --> 00:01:59.240
Yeah.

49
00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:01.040
And we were like, oh, wow.

50
00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:02.280
That felt good.

51
00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:02.960
Feels good.

52
00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:03.360
Yeah.

53
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:04.860
So we're getting off the wheel.

54
00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:09.840
And then I want to get us a gift that I was telling Jenna, and I'm sure you're going

55
00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:10.780
to be like, I don't want it.

56
00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:15.660
I want to get on a pillow or like some kind of embroidery, a little hamster on a wheel with

57
00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:16.820
a big X across it.

58
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:18.960
Why would I not want that?

59
00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:20.280
I want that.

60
00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:24.760
I want it to be like a visual reminder, because it's easy to climb on that wheel.

61
00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:25.520
It is.

62
00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:25.880
Right?

63
00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:26.600
Yes.

64
00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:27.040
Mm-hmm.

65
00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:27.740
All right.

66
00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:28.120
All right.

67
00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:29.340
Shared New Year's resolution.

68
00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.840
Well, listen, we are excited to be back.

69
00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:36.420
We're excited to be breaking down all of the episodes of the paper in the new year.

70
00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.580
But we have a new, what would you call it?

71
00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:39.440
More new things.

72
00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:41.040
We have a new format.

73
00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:42.220
Oh, yes.

74
00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:43.660
For the new year.

75
00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:48.960
We are going to kick off each episode with a chit-chat suggestion from one of you.

76
00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.040
Yeah, we are having so much fun.

77
00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:53.300
You guys have sent us really great topics.

78
00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:57.900
So let's kick off this episode with a suggestion from Stephanie A.

79
00:02:58.120 --> 00:02:59.680
in San Diego, California.

80
00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:01.960
Hi, office ladies.

81
00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:06.740
This is Stephanie in San Diego, California, originally from Ohio.

82
00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:07.580
Go Buckeyes.

83
00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:14.140
My question for you is, where is the worst place you have been stuck for a long time?

84
00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.280
Looking forward to hearing the answers.

85
00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:18.600
I knew mine right away.

86
00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:18.960
Same.

87
00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:20.040
Go.

88
00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:20.660
You go.

89
00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:21.240
Me go?

90
00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:21.980
Yeah, you go.

91
00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:23.200
Mine's kind of long.

92
00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:24.460
Mine's also long.

93
00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:25.000
Oh, no.

94
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.200
Well, these are clearly core memories for us.

95
00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:30.940
Stephanie, great question.

96
00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:38.040
Many, many, many years ago, when I was still married to James, we received a Christmas gift.

97
00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:39.160
Okay.

98
00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:44.400
And it was to go on an outing on a shark boat.

99
00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.280
Oh, my gosh.

100
00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:47.460
What's a shark boat?

101
00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:48.880
You get on a boat.

102
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:49.380
Uh-huh.

103
00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:52.680
Somewhere in, like, the Long Beach Harbor.

104
00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:53.260
Okay.

105
00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:58.840
And you go out to sea, and then you see sharks, I guess.

106
00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:00.880
Like they swim up by the boat.

107
00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.540
I think you go down in a cage.

108
00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:05.760
And then you can see sharks.

109
00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:06.460
Jenna.

110
00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:09.540
Let me tell you what happened, though.

111
00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:12.300
We loaded onto the boat.

112
00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:12.820
Okay.

113
00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:14.320
With enthusiasm.

114
00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:17.420
And we got out.

115
00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:20.060
Oh, we were 10 minutes on this boat.

116
00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:23.180
I got so, so seasick.

117
00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:29.980
Like, so seasick that I spent the whole trip inside the little galley of the boat.

118
00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:30.640
Oh, no.

119
00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:35.100
Curled up under the dining table, there was no Dramamine.

120
00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:36.160
There were no options.

121
00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:40.020
It was just me and my personal grit.

122
00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:41.780
And—

123
00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:43.020
Just muscling through it.

124
00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:47.280
That is a miserable feeling, that kind of nausea, that rocking back and forth.

125
00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:47.740
Yes.

126
00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:50.740
Because when you get out there, they stop the boat.

127
00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:50.980
Yeah.

128
00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:52.240
And so you're just rocking.

129
00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:53.080
Bobbing.

130
00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:54.140
For hours.

131
00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:55.020
Hours.

132
00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:59.260
And my kids know about the shark boat experience.

133
00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:05.340
Because I have said, like, as an anecdote, if I can survive the shark boat, I can survive anything.

134
00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:14.620
And when I was getting chemo and I said that I didn't feel good, my kids said, is it worse than the shark boat?

135
00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:15.160
Oh.

136
00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:17.260
Like, that's how, like—

137
00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:17.620
Right.

138
00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:20.240
—how awful the shark boat was.

139
00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:22.800
And I texted James about it.

140
00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:25.260
I said, do you mind if I tell the shark boat story?

141
00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:27.300
And also, I can't remember.

142
00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:29.640
Did you enjoy the shark boat?

143
00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:29.900
Yeah.

144
00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:31.080
Did he go in the cage?

145
00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:33.420
He—no, he didn't.

146
00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:36.480
He wrote me and he said, and he said I could quote him.

147
00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:39.900
He said, quote, it was one of the worst days of my life.

148
00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.020
Oh, no.

149
00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:42.560
He said I was miserable.

150
00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:45.660
I didn't throw up like nearly everyone else around me.

151
00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:48.000
And then I asked him about the cage.

152
00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:50.640
I said, did you see any sharks?

153
00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:51.600
I wouldn't know.

154
00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:54.060
I spent the whole trip under the table.

155
00:05:54.260 --> 00:06:01.700
He said they brought out a shark that was about nine inches long, and I pet it, and I felt terrible for it.

156
00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:04.400
That's the only f***ing shark we saw.

157
00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:06.140
Worst day of my life.

158
00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:07.980
Oh, that's Josh's worst nightmare.

159
00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:10.200
He gets so motion sick.

160
00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:11.280
He can't go on a boat.

161
00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:13.020
I have swam with sharks.

162
00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:13.860
You know that, though.

163
00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:14.340
Yes.

164
00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:14.680
Yeah.

165
00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:18.840
But they weren't like big, scary sharks, and they were well-fed.

166
00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:20.340
And Rashida and I swam with them.

167
00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:25.140
I mean, it sounds like the only shark that James saw was about the size of a large fish.

168
00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:25.840
Oh, no.

169
00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:27.040
Nine inches?

170
00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:28.100
I mean—

171
00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:31.300
That's—and you guys, it was all day on that boat.

172
00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:31.940
All day.

173
00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:32.400
That's horrible.

174
00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:33.100
Yeah.

175
00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:33.680
Oh.

176
00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:34.400
Yeah.

177
00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:38.140
I got through it by telling myself that time passes.

178
00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:38.700
Yeah.

179
00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:39.900
Time can't stop.

180
00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:41.340
This day will end.

181
00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:43.040
The sun will move through the sky.

182
00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:44.540
Night will come.

183
00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:47.220
The boat will dock, and I will get off.

184
00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:49.460
That was the mantra I played in my head.

185
00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:51.420
It was my own personal, the edge.

186
00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:54.520
I was just going to say, you're like singing Annie.

187
00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:56.880
The sun will come out tomorrow.

188
00:06:57.320 --> 00:06:57.680
Basically.

189
00:06:57.980 --> 00:06:58.600
Oh, my gosh.

190
00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:00.780
All right, that's the worst place I've ever been stuck.

191
00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:01.960
What about you?

192
00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:07.580
Well, Jenna, I actually was texting you from the worst place I've ever been stuck.

193
00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:10.220
So I'm going to jog your memory now.

194
00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:11.620
Was it—

195
00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:12.080
Well.

196
00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:17.800
It was the biggest snowstorm in North Texas in 21 years.

197
00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:21.580
It was December 24th, Christmas Eve, 2009.

198
00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:24.360
It's actually called the Christmas Eve blizzard.

199
00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:30.740
And I was flying back to see my family for the holidays with Isabel the morning of Christmas Eve.

200
00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:33.380
I think, Jenna, that year we worked right up into the holidays.

201
00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:35.900
So that was the day I could get out of town.

202
00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:38.520
And my dad said, well, there's a big storm coming in.

203
00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:39.560
So get the early flight.

204
00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:40.780
Get the earliest flight.

205
00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:47.580
Because once you land in Dallas, our family farm is a good two-and-a-half-hour drive from Dallas.

206
00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:50.820
So we're talking rural farm-to-market roads.

207
00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:52.700
And, you know, they don't go out and treat those roads.

208
00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:54.300
They don't put salt on them.

209
00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:55.120
They ice over.

210
00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:57.780
So my dad was like, we are going to need to get on the road.

211
00:07:58.420 --> 00:07:58.820
Okay.

212
00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:00.920
So we land.

213
00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:04.180
Temperatures drop to, like, their 20s.

214
00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:06.720
Winds kick up to, like, 60 miles an hour.

215
00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:09.700
It's snowing like crazy when we land.

216
00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:11.380
They close DFW Airport.

217
00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:12.900
Oh, I remember all this now.

218
00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:13.240
Yeah.

219
00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:14.680
No more flights going out.

220
00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:16.300
So we get in the car.

221
00:08:16.540 --> 00:08:20.100
And as we start to drive, it is just coming down and coming down.

222
00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:22.120
Isabel was only 19 months old.

223
00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:24.620
It was me, my mom, my dad, Isabel, and Warren.

224
00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:26.900
And it was like a whiteout.

225
00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:28.320
My dad could barely see.

226
00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:32.300
We were watching cars slide off the road into snowdrifts.

227
00:08:32.420 --> 00:08:33.180
I remember this.

228
00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:36.120
Hundreds of people were stranded in this storm.

229
00:08:36.680 --> 00:08:42.760
And, you know, these little towns did not have the resources to get this many people stranded out of the snow.

230
00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:45.620
So they called out to, like, local farmers.

231
00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:49.460
They were getting their tractors, their plows, trying to get people out.

232
00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:52.080
I ran out of everything in our diaper bag.

233
00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:53.820
We ran out of food.

234
00:08:54.020 --> 00:08:55.160
We were stuck.

235
00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:59.380
And we made it as far as outside of this town called Decatur.

236
00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:04.140
And we were at the bottom of this hill.

237
00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:10.900
And when you got to the other side, the farmers had been kind of successful clearing the road a little bit on the other side.

238
00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:12.300
But no one could get over the hill.

239
00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:17.340
And it was just like all of these cars slid off in the snow.

240
00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:18.780
And then you'd watch another one try.

241
00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:20.700
And my dad sat there.

242
00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:22.480
We were running out of gas.

243
00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:25.860
And he would turn the car off to save the gas.

244
00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:27.600
And we'd get really cold.

245
00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:29.440
We would be able to see our breath.

246
00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:30.980
And then he'd turn the car on for a little bit.

247
00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:31.620
We'd warm up.

248
00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:33.860
And we were doing this, like, intermittently.

249
00:09:34.300 --> 00:09:40.100
And just sitting there watching car after car try this hill and go into the snowbank.

250
00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:41.780
Now it's nightfall.

251
00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:44.180
Our windshield has frozen over.

252
00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:48.760
And I'm getting really worried because I have a 19-month-old.

253
00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:51.080
And I'm just like, it's cold.

254
00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:52.300
We're running out of gas.

255
00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:55.360
And my dad was like, we got to go for it.

256
00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:56.820
We got to go for the hill.

257
00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:58.040
We just have to.

258
00:09:58.940 --> 00:10:01.680
And we had no—my dad—oh, my gosh.

259
00:10:01.820 --> 00:10:05.180
My dad felt so bad because we never get this kind of weather there.

260
00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:07.480
He didn't have an ice scraper for the windshield.

261
00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:13.760
So he got out his country music CDs and busted them apart so they'd get sharp and jagged.

262
00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:19.340
And they got out there, him and Warren, like, scraping off the ice on the windshield.

263
00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:20.880
My dad slipped and fell.

264
00:10:21.020 --> 00:10:22.940
I was like, oh, my gosh, Dad, are you okay?

265
00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:30.220
And I'll never forget, my dad goes, I'm all right, but my dignity took a hit because he wiped out.

266
00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:33.640
Okay, I have to tell you, that's when I start texting you.

267
00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:34.780
I was like, Jenna, I'm worried.

268
00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:35.840
I'm worried.

269
00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:36.620
It is dark.

270
00:10:36.820 --> 00:10:38.600
People are stranded in their cars.

271
00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:40.340
And you said, Ange, I'm scared.

272
00:10:40.340 --> 00:10:45.740
I mean, as you're retelling this story, I am having all those feelings come back.

273
00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:50.100
And also, this is like—this is not a funny story.

274
00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:52.400
This is like a traumatic story.

275
00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:53.440
No, it's not.

276
00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:54.060
I'm sorry.

277
00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:55.000
But it's the—

278
00:10:55.000 --> 00:10:55.820
It's the truth.

279
00:10:55.880 --> 00:10:57.600
It is the worst place I've ever been stuck.

280
00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:06.200
And so then, while we're scraping the ice off the windshield, there's a young woman holding this little dog.

281
00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:08.220
And she's out in the snow.

282
00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:09.560
Her car's run out of gas.

283
00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:14.780
And she's going from car to car that's stranded, knocking on the windows to see if someone will take her in.

284
00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:18.660
And she's—I don't know, she's probably in her 20s, you know?

285
00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:20.700
My dad waves at her.

286
00:11:20.780 --> 00:11:23.140
He's like, we'll make room because we had a big SUV.

287
00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:28.320
So now this young gal gets in the car with us with her Chihuahua.

288
00:11:28.820 --> 00:11:31.240
And I remember the Chihuahua's name was Giselle.

289
00:11:31.460 --> 00:11:32.780
So now we're all in it.

290
00:11:32.780 --> 00:11:36.400
And there's an 18-wheeler that's in front of us that's going to try the hill.

291
00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:38.100
He starts to go up.

292
00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:39.740
He slides sideways off.

293
00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:41.220
And now it's our turn.

294
00:11:41.380 --> 00:11:44.940
You have to remind me, why have you not stopped for shelter?

295
00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:50.420
It's because there's just a vast nothingness between where you left and where you're going.

296
00:11:50.420 --> 00:11:56.860
No, once you pass Decatur, for those of you guys that drive north in Texas, there's nothing for a while, you know?

297
00:11:57.200 --> 00:11:58.100
And my—

298
00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:02.240
No, that's what it's like when you drive to Kirksville, Missouri, which is where I went to school.

299
00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:05.680
There's just like long stretches of there's no gas station.

300
00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:05.980
No.

301
00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:06.480
There's nothing.

302
00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:07.120
There's nothing.

303
00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:08.080
Just pasture.

304
00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:08.440
Yeah.

305
00:12:08.840 --> 00:12:12.660
Just acres of just land with nothing on it but some cows.

306
00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:16.920
So the 18-wheeler slides off, and I'm like, oh, my God.

307
00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:17.920
And now it's our turn.

308
00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:20.340
And I'm like, if we slide off, we're in a snowbank.

309
00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:22.040
Right now we're on a road.

310
00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:25.000
So my mom just starts praying.

311
00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:33.460
Even if we get to the other side of the hill, we didn't have a plan except in the neighboring little town, my parents knew a pastor that lived there.

312
00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:40.020
And he said if we could make it to the town, we could stay at their house for the night, him and his wife and their two kids.

313
00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:44.740
So we start—my dad, I mean, my dad was such a hero in this moment.

314
00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:47.640
He starts the SUV with everything he has.

315
00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:49.040
He's just like going to gun it.

316
00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:53.520
He guns it, and we're going up, and we are sliding left and right and sliding.

317
00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:55.160
And he's not turning into the slide.

318
00:12:55.160 --> 00:12:57.740
He's letting the car, like, just go with the slide.

319
00:12:58.140 --> 00:13:00.160
And there were a few times I was like, we're going to go off.

320
00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:00.700
We're going to go off.

321
00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:01.320
And we didn't.

322
00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:03.340
We didn't, and we made it to the top.

323
00:13:03.460 --> 00:13:09.080
I mean, you could have heard, like, it was like the winning touchdown at the Super Bowl.

324
00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:10.860
In that car, we were like, ah!

325
00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:15.500
And Isabel, poor Isabel, I just don't even know what she remembers about this.

326
00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:18.460
I asked her if she remembered, and she said she remembered snow.

327
00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:22.560
And we get to the top of the hill, and then we just slide.

328
00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:23.880
We just coast down.

329
00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:25.980
Little by little, we make it to this town.

330
00:13:26.180 --> 00:13:30.000
We can't even get down the road that the pastor and his family live on.

331
00:13:30.200 --> 00:13:32.340
But we get out, and we wade through the snow.

332
00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:33.320
And Giselle?

333
00:13:33.460 --> 00:13:34.200
And Giselle.

334
00:13:34.420 --> 00:13:36.660
Did she stay at the pastor's house, too?

335
00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:37.120
Yes.

336
00:13:37.300 --> 00:13:39.660
And I want to give a shout out to this pastor and his family.

337
00:13:39.820 --> 00:13:45.140
His name is Mike Sanders, and he and his wife, Andrea, were so lovely to me and my family.

338
00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:46.840
And I'm forever grateful.

339
00:13:46.840 --> 00:13:52.280
And on Christmas Eve night at 11 p.m., okay, we started our drive at 3 p.m.

340
00:13:52.660 --> 00:13:55.780
At 11 p.m., we arrive at this pastor's house.

341
00:13:55.940 --> 00:13:56.920
We're wading through the snow.

342
00:13:57.100 --> 00:14:04.060
We're, you know, I remember holding Isabel high up so she wouldn't get just damp from all the snow because it was waist high.

343
00:14:04.060 --> 00:14:05.980
And they took us in.

344
00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:07.080
We were starving.

345
00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:11.680
They fed five adults, a chihuahua, and a 19-month-old.

346
00:14:11.680 --> 00:14:13.800
And we woke up Christmas morning with this family.

347
00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:24.340
And finally, that young woman who got in the car with us was able to reach her dad because, you know, she'd run out of cell service and everything.

348
00:14:24.460 --> 00:14:26.780
Her family, she was driving home for the holidays.

349
00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:28.180
They didn't know where she was.

350
00:14:28.180 --> 00:14:29.620
They knew she was stuck in this storm.

351
00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:35.960
Her dad and her brothers, because the roads still weren't cleared, slowly made it to the pastor's house on Christmas Day.

352
00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:41.940
And when he arrived and he saw his daughter, he hugged her and began to cry.

353
00:14:42.440 --> 00:14:46.580
And then he turned to my dad, choked up, and shook my dad's hand and said,

354
00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:47.060
Thank you.

355
00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:47.820
Thank you.

356
00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:49.480
Not a dry eye.

357
00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:50.520
We all were sobbing.

358
00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:53.640
And then she was able to leave and go to her family's home.

359
00:14:53.640 --> 00:15:00.760
We didn't make it to our farm until Christmas Day, late in the day, because we had to go so slow.

360
00:15:00.760 --> 00:15:02.140
But the roads were better.

361
00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:03.120
It was melting.

362
00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:05.580
That's the longest I've ever been stuck anywhere.

363
00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:11.220
I mean, this also sounds like an answer to the question, what's your most memorable Christmas?

364
00:15:11.660 --> 00:15:14.260
I mean, that's right up there.

365
00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:15.240
Wow.

366
00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:23.620
Well, I think that if I had to choose, I would get back on the shark boat rather than do your trip.

367
00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:24.440
Your adventure.

368
00:15:24.980 --> 00:15:25.980
Oh, gosh.

369
00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:29.140
Because, and I've never chosen the shark boat.

370
00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:30.420
Over something else.

371
00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:31.380
Over something else.

372
00:15:31.740 --> 00:15:34.580
I've never, the shark boat has never won anything.

373
00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:36.700
Well, I mean, yeah.

374
00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:37.700
Because, you know why?

375
00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:39.720
Because you weren't just stuck.

376
00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:41.220
You were terrified.

377
00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:42.300
We were scared.

378
00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:44.020
I wasn't scared on the shark boat.

379
00:15:44.280 --> 00:15:45.120
I was just miserable.

380
00:15:45.860 --> 00:15:46.120
Yeah.

381
00:15:46.520 --> 00:15:46.900
Wow.

382
00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:49.440
Well, Stephanie, thank you for your question.

383
00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:53.360
I mean, that was a real, real, like, zing dinger.

384
00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:54.600
I don't know the expression.

385
00:15:54.940 --> 00:15:57.000
But that was my honest answer.

386
00:15:57.100 --> 00:15:59.020
I was like, I mean, that's it.

387
00:15:59.140 --> 00:16:00.680
That's the worst place I've ever been stuck.

388
00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:02.760
Well, listen, why don't we take a break?

389
00:16:02.760 --> 00:16:07.400
And when we come back, we're going to break down The Paper, Season 1, Episode 2.

390
00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:09.000
Where no one is stuck anywhere.

391
00:16:09.420 --> 00:16:09.580
True.

392
00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:11.440
Right.

393
00:16:21.550 --> 00:16:23.210
We are back.

394
00:16:23.570 --> 00:16:31.550
And we will be talking about The Paper, Season 1, Episode 2, titled The Five W's.

395
00:16:31.690 --> 00:16:32.030
Mm-hmm.

396
00:16:32.030 --> 00:16:38.110
This episode was written by Greg Daniels and Michael Komen and directed by Ken Quapis.

397
00:16:38.430 --> 00:16:39.790
Aw, our favorite.

398
00:16:40.490 --> 00:16:42.150
Let me give you a summary.

399
00:16:42.490 --> 00:16:43.790
It's been a long time.

400
00:16:44.050 --> 00:16:45.970
Were you excited to give a summary?

401
00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:51.570
I was excited to give a summary, but I'm only doing one fast fact.

402
00:16:51.870 --> 00:16:52.890
And that's okay.

403
00:16:53.090 --> 00:16:57.470
Moving forward, these breakdowns will contain a single fast fact.

404
00:16:57.670 --> 00:16:58.210
Not three.

405
00:16:58.510 --> 00:16:59.390
Here's the thing.

406
00:16:59.530 --> 00:17:01.090
We are not on the paper.

407
00:17:01.430 --> 00:17:01.710
Right.

408
00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:06.570
But we know so many people who are working on the paper, so they're giving us little,

409
00:17:06.570 --> 00:17:10.250
you know, behind-the-scenes details, and I think it's okay that you're only doing one

410
00:17:10.250 --> 00:17:10.770
fast fact.

411
00:17:10.930 --> 00:17:11.530
Thank you, lady.

412
00:17:11.650 --> 00:17:12.310
Thank you.

413
00:17:12.670 --> 00:17:14.050
Well, here is your summary.

414
00:17:14.510 --> 00:17:20.590
Ned is struggling to stitch together his first issue of the floundering Toledo Truth Teller

415
00:17:20.590 --> 00:17:21.250
newspaper.

416
00:17:22.510 --> 00:17:28.690
Esmeralda sabotages Ned's efforts by killing their wire service and doctoring his first letter

417
00:17:28.690 --> 00:17:29.550
to the readers.

418
00:17:29.630 --> 00:17:31.390
She still has it out for him.

419
00:17:31.390 --> 00:17:31.950
Well, yeah.

420
00:17:32.130 --> 00:17:32.650
She's bitter.

421
00:17:32.970 --> 00:17:33.270
Yeah.

422
00:17:33.430 --> 00:17:34.910
He's kind of replaced her.

423
00:17:35.110 --> 00:17:36.030
Yes, for sure.

424
00:17:36.130 --> 00:17:36.290
Yeah.

425
00:17:36.550 --> 00:17:38.110
He also stole her office.

426
00:17:38.330 --> 00:17:39.810
That wasn't necessary, I think.

427
00:17:39.810 --> 00:17:40.410
I agree.

428
00:17:40.770 --> 00:17:40.890
Yeah.

429
00:17:41.410 --> 00:17:45.630
The staff of the paper become reporters, but no one really writes anything.

430
00:17:45.630 --> 00:17:53.490
He has to turn this flop into a feature by writing a blunt and very earnest letter, owning that

431
00:17:53.490 --> 00:17:58.130
it was a quiet news day, and rebooting the paper's promise to tell the truth.

432
00:17:58.890 --> 00:17:59.930
There you go.

433
00:18:00.370 --> 00:18:00.850
All right.

434
00:18:00.970 --> 00:18:07.730
Fast fact number one, we are watching the broadcast version of these episodes of the paper.

435
00:18:07.910 --> 00:18:13.070
You know they live on Peacock, but now they're also living on NBC Monday nights at 830.

436
00:18:13.830 --> 00:18:19.710
And I thought to myself, wait a second, they were really long on Peacock, but to be on broadcast,

437
00:18:20.010 --> 00:18:21.390
they can only be 22 minutes.

438
00:18:21.890 --> 00:18:22.130
Yeah.

439
00:18:22.130 --> 00:18:24.110
I asked Dave Rogers about it.

440
00:18:24.290 --> 00:18:27.070
He said, yeah, we had to cut them down.

441
00:18:27.230 --> 00:18:33.770
He said most of the episodes were like in the 28 to 29 minute range, and he had to spend his

442
00:18:33.770 --> 00:18:38.610
entire summer cutting them all down to 21 minutes and 30 seconds.

443
00:18:38.790 --> 00:18:42.490
It was like reverse supersizing that he's doing.

444
00:18:42.650 --> 00:18:45.790
He's, you know, for The Office, he's doing the supersized episodes for Peacock.

445
00:18:45.950 --> 00:18:47.950
He had to do like reverse supersize.

446
00:18:48.110 --> 00:18:48.750
My gosh.

447
00:18:48.750 --> 00:18:49.630
What would you call that?

448
00:18:50.370 --> 00:18:52.050
Reverse supersize, I guess.

449
00:18:52.310 --> 00:18:53.290
So, yes.

450
00:18:53.350 --> 00:18:58.530
He said that because so much time had to come out of each episode, both Greg and Michael

451
00:18:58.530 --> 00:19:05.310
really wanted to be involved in the process, and they each had their own way of trimming

452
00:19:05.310 --> 00:19:06.010
the episode.

453
00:19:06.210 --> 00:19:10.130
He said Greg would take the broadcast scripts.

454
00:19:10.390 --> 00:19:16.050
So like after a show is broadcast, they turn it into a script because then it will incorporate

455
00:19:16.050 --> 00:19:20.430
all of the improvs and the way it was edited.

456
00:19:20.690 --> 00:19:21.130
That's right.

457
00:19:21.250 --> 00:19:23.110
It's like the transcripts you find online.

458
00:19:23.290 --> 00:19:23.690
Exactly.

459
00:19:24.050 --> 00:19:25.910
They form those after the episode airs.

460
00:19:26.030 --> 00:19:31.650
So Greg would take the broadcast scripts and he would do what they call box cuts.

461
00:19:32.330 --> 00:19:35.570
That's where he would just put a giant box around certain scenes.

462
00:19:35.570 --> 00:19:40.770
And then if there were other little bits that related to that scene, they would cut those

463
00:19:40.770 --> 00:19:41.270
out too.

464
00:19:41.570 --> 00:19:48.710
But I guess Michael Komen would take the Peacock cuts and actually use editing software where

465
00:19:48.710 --> 00:19:53.810
he pulled out little pieces of scenes and little pieces of dialogue to try to get down to the

466
00:19:53.810 --> 00:19:55.390
21 minutes, 30 seconds.

467
00:19:55.390 --> 00:20:01.170
This is very interesting because it also is a little bit of a generational choice.

468
00:20:01.870 --> 00:20:06.310
Greg is clearly paper and pen, and Michael is computer.

469
00:20:07.390 --> 00:20:14.210
Dave said he would kind of use both of those as quote-unquote notes to determine how he would

470
00:20:14.210 --> 00:20:15.810
cut the episode down.

471
00:20:15.950 --> 00:20:20.870
But he said there were a couple times early on that Michael would suggest doing a different

472
00:20:20.870 --> 00:20:23.770
take of a scene because it was shorter or faster.

473
00:20:24.350 --> 00:20:32.470
And he and Greg had to explain that since this is a documentary, there aren't multiple takes.

474
00:20:32.930 --> 00:20:33.450
Right.

475
00:20:33.670 --> 00:20:37.850
So if a take or an angle lives in the original episode.

476
00:20:38.150 --> 00:20:38.730
That's it.

477
00:20:38.870 --> 00:20:40.250
It can't be changed.

478
00:20:40.510 --> 00:20:44.690
It can be removed, but it can't be a new version of that same scene.

479
00:20:44.890 --> 00:20:45.410
Exactly.

480
00:20:45.950 --> 00:20:46.990
So there you have it.

481
00:20:47.070 --> 00:20:48.770
That's your single fast fact.

482
00:20:49.030 --> 00:20:49.870
I like it.

483
00:20:50.070 --> 00:20:51.610
Well, let's get into the episode.

484
00:20:52.250 --> 00:20:57.270
This episode opens with Ned welcoming everyone into the conference room.

485
00:20:57.910 --> 00:21:03.270
Ned, you remember, is the brand new editor-in-chief of the Truth Teller paper, a newspaper that

486
00:21:03.270 --> 00:21:05.550
up until now has written no original stories.

487
00:21:05.870 --> 00:21:09.990
Yeah, they would just pull clips off of other news sites, but now they have to do it all

488
00:21:09.990 --> 00:21:10.570
on their own.

489
00:21:10.790 --> 00:21:13.150
Did you notice what the sign said on the door?

490
00:21:13.350 --> 00:21:13.930
I did.

491
00:21:13.930 --> 00:21:16.070
There's a handmade sign on the door.

492
00:21:16.190 --> 00:21:21.290
As you walk into the conference room, it says, Truth Teller staff meeting, 9 a.m.

493
00:21:22.030 --> 00:21:22.890
9 a.m.

494
00:21:22.910 --> 00:21:24.110
I was like, that's kind of early.

495
00:21:24.410 --> 00:21:26.190
I mean, he has to get going.

496
00:21:26.330 --> 00:21:26.790
He does.

497
00:21:27.510 --> 00:21:30.070
Everyone is in the conference room except for two people.

498
00:21:30.730 --> 00:21:31.950
Oscar is not there.

499
00:21:32.110 --> 00:21:32.270
Yep.

500
00:21:32.370 --> 00:21:34.150
And Esmeralda is not there.

501
00:21:34.590 --> 00:21:39.030
And Ned starts by asking, how many people have written for a newspaper before?

502
00:21:39.370 --> 00:21:40.170
Only mayor.

503
00:21:40.170 --> 00:21:40.990
Only mayor.

504
00:21:41.310 --> 00:21:44.150
And everyone else's experience is pretty thin.

505
00:21:44.390 --> 00:21:47.650
Someone's like, oh, I have a text thread.

506
00:21:47.810 --> 00:21:48.110
Really?

507
00:21:48.870 --> 00:21:50.350
That's their writing experience.

508
00:21:50.710 --> 00:21:50.850
I've tweeted.

509
00:21:51.350 --> 00:21:53.250
It's like the experience is real slim.

510
00:21:53.890 --> 00:21:58.110
Ned is now going to share his favorite issues of the Toledo Truth Teller.

511
00:21:58.730 --> 00:22:03.290
He's taken like these newspapers, these front pages, they've been framed.

512
00:22:03.490 --> 00:22:06.290
They're like antiques that hang in the hallway of the building.

513
00:22:06.290 --> 00:22:09.430
And he brings his favorites into the room.

514
00:22:09.430 --> 00:22:12.590
These are the headlines on his favorite issues.

515
00:22:13.210 --> 00:22:18.890
Shepard Orbits the Earth, Dr. Salk Cures Polio, and Victory in Europe.

516
00:22:19.090 --> 00:22:20.490
I looked up the headlines.

517
00:22:20.770 --> 00:22:22.070
I figured you would.

518
00:22:22.510 --> 00:22:25.970
So Alan Shepard did do a suborbit of the Earth in 1961.

519
00:22:26.310 --> 00:22:29.770
Dr. Salk created the polio vaccine in 1955.

520
00:22:30.250 --> 00:22:33.990
And Victory in Europe was declared in 1945.

521
00:22:33.990 --> 00:22:36.370
I was like, how old is this paper?

522
00:22:37.090 --> 00:22:39.310
These headlines go back to 1945.

523
00:22:39.930 --> 00:22:43.250
I mean, you remember what the printing press looked like.

524
00:22:43.550 --> 00:22:45.070
It was in black and white.

525
00:22:45.250 --> 00:22:45.530
Oh, yeah.

526
00:22:45.650 --> 00:22:46.190
That's right.

527
00:22:46.930 --> 00:22:51.130
Well, then I looked up my hometown paper of the St. Louis Post-Dispatch.

528
00:22:51.310 --> 00:22:51.570
Yeah.

529
00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:57.810
The first issue of the St. Louis Post-Dispatch was December 12th, 1878.

530
00:22:58.210 --> 00:22:59.350
You go, St. Louis.

531
00:23:00.510 --> 00:23:00.990
1878.

532
00:23:01.970 --> 00:23:03.830
I saw a picture of the front page.

533
00:23:03.910 --> 00:23:05.630
It was almost all ads.

534
00:23:05.930 --> 00:23:06.410
Hmm.

535
00:23:06.590 --> 00:23:12.150
Were early newspapers just—it looked kind of like just an ad section.

536
00:23:12.430 --> 00:23:15.050
And the, oh, the ads lady, you would have loved it.

537
00:23:15.110 --> 00:23:15.650
Oh, I bet.

538
00:23:15.650 --> 00:23:20.030
It was like needlepoint napkins now on sale.

539
00:23:20.250 --> 00:23:20.530
Mm-hmm.

540
00:23:20.770 --> 00:23:21.650
Lace gloves.

541
00:23:21.790 --> 00:23:22.030
Yes.

542
00:23:22.150 --> 00:23:22.430
Half off.

543
00:23:22.610 --> 00:23:23.250
Lace gloves.

544
00:23:23.650 --> 00:23:24.050
Yes.

545
00:23:24.710 --> 00:23:27.790
Well, Ken, he's kind of like one of the boss guys over everything.

546
00:23:28.170 --> 00:23:29.390
He's this British guy.

547
00:23:29.670 --> 00:23:31.790
He's going to come in and stir up some s**t.

548
00:23:31.990 --> 00:23:37.890
And I just want you guys to know, I have a few people in this episode that I'm calling s**t stirrers.

549
00:23:38.210 --> 00:23:38.610
Oh.

550
00:23:38.810 --> 00:23:39.050
Mm-hmm.

551
00:23:39.050 --> 00:23:39.850
Is he one of them?

552
00:23:39.910 --> 00:23:40.690
He's one of them.

553
00:23:40.950 --> 00:23:43.930
And I had to call a bulls**t card on him right out of the gate.

554
00:23:43.970 --> 00:23:44.050
Oh, ready?

555
00:23:44.190 --> 00:23:44.430
Yeah.

556
00:23:44.430 --> 00:23:45.010
I'm sorry.

557
00:23:45.010 --> 00:23:45.790
What is it?

558
00:23:46.030 --> 00:23:49.030
Well, Ken comes in the room, and he's like, I'm sorry.

559
00:23:49.230 --> 00:23:53.690
You can't use these framed newspapers in your conference room meeting.

560
00:23:53.810 --> 00:23:54.810
I'm going to take them all back.

561
00:23:55.310 --> 00:23:55.750
Why?

562
00:23:55.950 --> 00:23:56.670
Why, Ken?

563
00:23:56.970 --> 00:23:57.350
Why?

564
00:23:57.590 --> 00:24:03.250
Why can't he show these framed, like, newspapers to his staff?

565
00:24:03.490 --> 00:24:04.390
Oh, I'm sorry.

566
00:24:04.550 --> 00:24:06.210
He's got to go hang them back in the hallway.

567
00:24:06.410 --> 00:24:06.850
Okay, great.

568
00:24:06.970 --> 00:24:08.530
Then everyone can just walk to the hallway.

569
00:24:08.890 --> 00:24:09.330
Right?

570
00:24:09.590 --> 00:24:09.850
Really?

571
00:24:10.270 --> 00:24:12.470
I guess he was worried about them getting damaged.

572
00:24:12.470 --> 00:24:15.090
But did you see the person who drops them on the floor?

573
00:24:15.590 --> 00:24:16.030
Ken.

574
00:24:16.150 --> 00:24:16.370
It's Ken.

575
00:24:16.590 --> 00:24:17.090
I know.

576
00:24:17.310 --> 00:24:18.390
At two minutes.

577
00:24:18.810 --> 00:24:18.860
I know.

578
00:24:18.860 --> 00:24:21.770
As he's leaving the conference room, he drops them on the floor.

579
00:24:22.090 --> 00:24:22.250
Yeah.

580
00:24:22.250 --> 00:24:23.130
And guess what?

581
00:24:23.170 --> 00:24:24.290
I have a tidbit about that.

582
00:24:24.410 --> 00:24:24.710
Okay.

583
00:24:25.130 --> 00:24:30.310
So when we shared that we were going to be breaking down these episodes, the amazing Chelsea Fry,

584
00:24:30.470 --> 00:24:34.670
who plays Mare, she reached out to us, and she said she was so excited.

585
00:24:34.670 --> 00:24:36.570
And she said, ask me any questions.

586
00:24:37.170 --> 00:24:42.810
So I asked her if she had any memories from this episode, and she said this conference room meeting.

587
00:24:43.070 --> 00:24:48.530
She said it was essentially the first conference room meeting where everybody was all together,

588
00:24:48.530 --> 00:24:50.010
and they were so nervous.

589
00:24:50.270 --> 00:24:52.430
They felt like there was a lot to live up to.

590
00:24:52.770 --> 00:24:58.010
So she said that when Tim Key, who plays Ken, left with all the framed articles,

591
00:24:58.830 --> 00:25:01.190
he dropped them, and it was an accident.

592
00:25:01.410 --> 00:25:02.810
No, it's so perfect.

593
00:25:02.830 --> 00:25:03.770
It's so perfect.

594
00:25:03.770 --> 00:25:05.610
And she said it broke the tension.

595
00:25:05.870 --> 00:25:09.150
Everybody couldn't stop laughing, and then they left it in.

596
00:25:09.490 --> 00:25:10.990
I think that's great.

597
00:25:11.570 --> 00:25:13.270
Aw, Chelsea, great tidbit.

598
00:25:13.590 --> 00:25:16.610
I do have a favorite moment in this conference room scene,

599
00:25:17.210 --> 00:25:21.250
and it's when Barry lights a cigarette, and he smokes a cigarette.

600
00:25:21.430 --> 00:25:21.850
I know.

601
00:25:22.270 --> 00:25:24.330
That's longer in the Peacock version.

602
00:25:24.470 --> 00:25:24.710
Yeah.

603
00:25:24.810 --> 00:25:27.030
He's, like, smoking a cigarette through the whole meeting.

604
00:25:27.170 --> 00:25:28.250
No one says anything.

605
00:25:28.430 --> 00:25:29.930
No one's like, do you mind?

606
00:25:29.930 --> 00:25:30.930
Like, the smoke?

607
00:25:31.170 --> 00:25:31.470
Nothing.

608
00:25:31.850 --> 00:25:32.230
Yes.

609
00:25:32.230 --> 00:25:32.290
Yes.

610
00:25:32.430 --> 00:25:37.250
And by the way, after Ken leaves, this conference room meeting just sort of ends.

611
00:25:37.690 --> 00:25:41.650
Yeah, it just sort of ends, and everyone kind of goes back to their desks, I guess.

612
00:25:42.250 --> 00:25:42.470
Yes.

613
00:25:43.290 --> 00:25:47.970
Ned is going to go up to Mare at her desk, and they're excited.

614
00:25:47.970 --> 00:25:49.410
They're going to start this first issue.

615
00:25:49.550 --> 00:25:52.250
They're going to go to the AP Wire and start pulling articles.

616
00:25:52.570 --> 00:25:52.910
Yes.

617
00:25:52.910 --> 00:25:59.050
And then Ned spies a tin of popcorn under Mare's desk and is like, hey, can I have some of that?

618
00:25:59.170 --> 00:26:00.330
And she's like, yeah, sure.

619
00:26:00.710 --> 00:26:01.770
And here's the thing.

620
00:26:02.190 --> 00:26:05.510
There would have been a Mare talking head right after this.

621
00:26:05.670 --> 00:26:10.450
Like, as soon as Ned starts diving in that popcorn tin, Mare would have said something I

622
00:26:10.450 --> 00:26:11.190
thought was hilarious.

623
00:26:11.270 --> 00:26:12.830
It's still in the Peacock version.

624
00:26:12.830 --> 00:26:14.550
It's not in the NBC broadcaSt.

625
00:26:15.050 --> 00:26:16.950
But I want you to hear about this popcorn.

626
00:26:17.270 --> 00:26:18.710
It brought back some memories for me.

627
00:26:19.570 --> 00:26:21.230
Hey, can I have some of this?

628
00:26:21.830 --> 00:26:22.530
Yeah, sure.

629
00:26:22.550 --> 00:26:22.670
Yeah.

630
00:26:22.830 --> 00:26:23.130
Dive in.

631
00:26:23.410 --> 00:26:23.830
Looks good.

632
00:26:25.570 --> 00:26:31.390
That popcorn was a gift to Maria, who had my desk before me, from Enrique, who used

633
00:26:31.390 --> 00:26:32.090
to work in sales.

634
00:26:32.350 --> 00:26:33.490
They have two kids now.

635
00:26:34.870 --> 00:26:35.390
Yes.

636
00:26:35.690 --> 00:26:39.810
This popcorn, it's one of those giant tins that's like divided in three.

637
00:26:40.050 --> 00:26:40.210
Yeah.

638
00:26:40.790 --> 00:26:44.050
And Ned's going to eat it through this entire episode.

639
00:26:44.050 --> 00:26:46.110
You see him carry this tin around.

640
00:26:46.290 --> 00:26:47.950
He's like basically like—

641
00:26:47.950 --> 00:26:48.650
Stress eating.

642
00:26:48.830 --> 00:26:49.090
Yes.

643
00:26:49.250 --> 00:26:49.490
Yes.

644
00:26:49.930 --> 00:26:53.410
He's stress eating maybe four-year-old popcorn.

645
00:26:53.830 --> 00:26:55.370
I mean, they have two kids?

646
00:26:55.510 --> 00:26:55.990
Two kids.

647
00:26:56.130 --> 00:26:57.630
Six-year-old popcorn?

648
00:26:57.930 --> 00:27:02.630
I mean, they had to fall in love, get married, and have two children, and not work there anymore.

649
00:27:02.850 --> 00:27:07.270
It reminded me when Ed ate those Twizzlers that had been on the set for like four years.

650
00:27:07.590 --> 00:27:09.010
What is that story?

651
00:27:09.390 --> 00:27:12.350
Don't you remember we had all those—were they Twizzlers or Red Vines?

652
00:27:12.350 --> 00:27:13.150
I can't remember.

653
00:27:13.250 --> 00:27:13.750
In the kitchen.

654
00:27:13.950 --> 00:27:15.930
And they'd been there for years.

655
00:27:15.930 --> 00:27:17.690
Oh, the big tub of Red Vines.

656
00:27:17.770 --> 00:27:18.210
Yes.

657
00:27:18.650 --> 00:27:20.250
On the set of The Office in the kitchen.

658
00:27:20.250 --> 00:27:21.530
And they were there for years.

659
00:27:21.530 --> 00:27:26.870
And then when Ed was new to the show, one day I saw him open it up and take one out.

660
00:27:26.970 --> 00:27:30.190
I'm like, but they've been here a while.

661
00:27:30.430 --> 00:27:31.650
He'd already eaten like half of it.

662
00:27:31.810 --> 00:27:33.830
I think that they last forever.

663
00:27:34.330 --> 00:27:35.410
I think they might.

664
00:27:35.650 --> 00:27:37.210
I do have a question for you.

665
00:27:37.330 --> 00:27:37.910
What is it?

666
00:27:38.170 --> 00:27:39.650
Are you Red Vines or Twizzlers?

667
00:27:40.190 --> 00:27:41.670
Oh, this is tough.

668
00:27:41.950 --> 00:27:42.130
Yeah.

669
00:27:42.930 --> 00:27:44.730
I am Red Vines at the movies.

670
00:27:44.850 --> 00:27:45.290
Okay.

671
00:27:45.910 --> 00:27:48.530
But I am Twizzlers at Lake of the Ozarks.

672
00:27:48.850 --> 00:27:49.050
What?

673
00:27:49.050 --> 00:27:54.910
My whole childhood, my mom would buy a pack of Twizzlers, which she calls liquish.

674
00:27:55.730 --> 00:27:56.250
Liquish?

675
00:27:56.490 --> 00:27:56.710
Yeah.

676
00:27:56.870 --> 00:27:58.010
Do you want some liquish?

677
00:27:58.190 --> 00:27:59.030
This is adorable.

678
00:27:59.470 --> 00:28:00.830
She doesn't call it licorice.

679
00:28:01.590 --> 00:28:02.790
But she would buy—

680
00:28:02.790 --> 00:28:05.030
Also, it's not liquish or licorice.

681
00:28:05.210 --> 00:28:06.290
Isn't it licorice?

682
00:28:06.410 --> 00:28:07.430
It's Red Licorice.

683
00:28:07.710 --> 00:28:08.230
Is it?

684
00:28:08.410 --> 00:28:09.150
I think so.

685
00:28:09.350 --> 00:28:09.590
Oh.

686
00:28:09.590 --> 00:28:14.810
But she would buy a big pack when we would go down to the lake for a week, and you could

687
00:28:14.810 --> 00:28:18.330
just go in the cabinet whenever you wanted, and you could take one.

688
00:28:18.490 --> 00:28:23.430
Because there's no—like, the cool thing about Lake of the Ozarks and my childhood was

689
00:28:23.430 --> 00:28:25.690
that you could just eat chips and—

690
00:28:25.690 --> 00:28:26.430
Different rules.

691
00:28:26.650 --> 00:28:26.990
Yes.

692
00:28:27.390 --> 00:28:28.950
Oreos and red licorice.

693
00:28:29.230 --> 00:28:30.410
This is like what I call—

694
00:28:30.410 --> 00:28:30.950
Willy-nilly.

695
00:28:31.110 --> 00:28:31.770
Plane travel.

696
00:28:32.050 --> 00:28:35.290
Like, we get to the airport, the kids can get whatever snack they want for the plane.

697
00:28:35.370 --> 00:28:35.550
Right.

698
00:28:35.690 --> 00:28:36.110
You know?

699
00:28:36.310 --> 00:28:36.490
Yes.

700
00:28:36.510 --> 00:28:38.250
And can get Skittles, whatever.

701
00:28:38.310 --> 00:28:39.090
It's the plane.

702
00:28:39.090 --> 00:28:39.770
What are you?

703
00:28:40.050 --> 00:28:40.990
I'm Red Vines.

704
00:28:41.130 --> 00:28:41.390
Mm-hmm.

705
00:28:41.590 --> 00:28:41.890
Mm-hmm.

706
00:28:42.190 --> 00:28:42.370
Yeah.

707
00:28:42.710 --> 00:28:44.810
Well, Ned is old popcorn tin.

708
00:28:44.990 --> 00:28:46.150
Super old popcorn tin.

709
00:28:46.350 --> 00:28:53.090
I asked Chelsea about the popcorn, because as I was watching him eat this popcorn throughout

710
00:28:53.090 --> 00:28:57.790
the whole episode, I was like, did anybody besides Donal eat the popcorn?

711
00:28:57.950 --> 00:28:59.130
Because I just had a hunch.

712
00:28:59.430 --> 00:28:59.590
Yeah.

713
00:29:00.030 --> 00:29:04.210
Just like what you're talking about, Ed, eating food, and I know BJ and I would eat

714
00:29:04.210 --> 00:29:05.590
prop food that wasn't ours.

715
00:29:05.890 --> 00:29:06.170
Yeah.

716
00:29:06.170 --> 00:29:11.870
She said, oh, my God, yes, she ate so much of it that she was sick by the end of the week.

717
00:29:12.470 --> 00:29:15.830
She said Taylor from Props had to refill it constantly.

718
00:29:16.810 --> 00:29:17.210
Okay.

719
00:29:17.210 --> 00:29:21.250
Well, I was very curious about this popcorn tin as well, and I reached out to the prop

720
00:29:21.250 --> 00:29:23.310
master for the paper, Scott McGinnis.

721
00:29:23.430 --> 00:29:24.110
We met him.

722
00:29:24.230 --> 00:29:24.470
Okay.

723
00:29:24.510 --> 00:29:24.910
Remember?

724
00:29:25.110 --> 00:29:25.250
Yes.

725
00:29:25.250 --> 00:29:25.790
When we were on set.

726
00:29:26.230 --> 00:29:27.750
And I asked him these questions.

727
00:29:28.010 --> 00:29:30.630
Scott, did the actors all really eat the popcorn?

728
00:29:30.810 --> 00:29:32.370
It looked like a lot of popcorn.

729
00:29:32.370 --> 00:29:35.970
Where did you get it from, what were the flavors, and was there a spit bucket?

730
00:29:36.350 --> 00:29:37.410
What are the answers?

731
00:29:37.730 --> 00:29:38.730
Here's what Scott said.

732
00:29:39.030 --> 00:29:42.890
The popcorn and tin were from Shirley's, a popular Ohio brand.

733
00:29:43.390 --> 00:29:44.630
Oh, I love this.

734
00:29:44.770 --> 00:29:45.030
Yeah.

735
00:29:45.130 --> 00:29:46.990
And he sent me the link so I can share it.

736
00:29:47.210 --> 00:29:51.890
He said, the actors ate quite a bit, but mostly Donal, who goes full into everything.

737
00:29:52.230 --> 00:29:53.570
Sounds like rain, doesn't it?

738
00:29:53.710 --> 00:29:53.830
Mm-hmm.

739
00:29:53.830 --> 00:29:54.030
Mm-hmm.

740
00:29:54.030 --> 00:29:58.550
Scott also said, the most popular flavor was, any guesses?

741
00:29:58.850 --> 00:29:59.230
Cheese.

742
00:29:59.570 --> 00:30:00.530
White cheddar.

743
00:30:01.030 --> 00:30:01.330
Oh!

744
00:30:01.830 --> 00:30:05.150
He said, it's good popcorn, and I don't remember needing a spit bucket.

745
00:30:06.850 --> 00:30:08.650
I like this inside info.

746
00:30:08.970 --> 00:30:11.390
Here's what's going to happen next in the episode.

747
00:30:11.710 --> 00:30:15.750
Mare and Ned discover that the wire service has been canceled.

748
00:30:16.030 --> 00:30:17.430
They can't get any stories.

749
00:30:18.050 --> 00:30:18.210
Yeah.

750
00:30:18.210 --> 00:30:25.310
As Marelda comes by, and in her very passive-aggressive way, she's like, oh, yes, I canceled the wire

751
00:30:25.310 --> 00:30:30.970
service because you so brilliantly declared that we would be writing original news stories.

752
00:30:31.290 --> 00:30:31.490
Yeah.

753
00:30:31.890 --> 00:30:33.090
This was your idea.

754
00:30:33.350 --> 00:30:34.730
Yeah, I did this for you.

755
00:30:34.950 --> 00:30:35.350
Mm-hmm.

756
00:30:35.930 --> 00:30:37.750
And he's like, what the f***?

757
00:30:37.930 --> 00:30:38.230
Yeah.

758
00:30:38.470 --> 00:30:42.790
And she's very sabotage-y, and then she mentions this lunch that they have.

759
00:30:43.190 --> 00:30:43.710
Yes.

760
00:30:44.370 --> 00:30:46.010
She's like, I'll see you at lunch.

761
00:30:46.010 --> 00:30:51.550
And then Ned panics, and he calls everybody back into the conference room because now

762
00:30:51.550 --> 00:30:55.850
they have to go get news stories today, right now.

763
00:30:55.970 --> 00:30:58.230
The deadline for printing is 6 p.m.

764
00:30:58.570 --> 00:30:58.910
Yes.

765
00:30:59.230 --> 00:31:02.350
Here are the news stories that they're going to chase down.

766
00:31:02.730 --> 00:31:04.670
This is all they could come up with.

767
00:31:04.890 --> 00:31:05.150
Mm-hmm.

768
00:31:06.050 --> 00:31:09.190
Adalola couldn't find a parking space at the dog park.

769
00:31:09.350 --> 00:31:10.230
They're on it.

770
00:31:10.370 --> 00:31:11.570
They're going to get to the bottom of that.

771
00:31:11.630 --> 00:31:13.930
Who's taking up all these extra parking spaces?

772
00:31:14.250 --> 00:31:14.730
Yes.

773
00:31:14.730 --> 00:31:20.470
There's a water main break in town, so Mare and Barry are going to go find out what's

774
00:31:20.470 --> 00:31:21.410
going on with that.

